WEBVTT

00:00:01.710 --> 00:00:02.746
Hi, I'm Cleat!

00:00:02.746 --> 00:00:05.509
Your sampler and your learner never quite agree.

00:00:05.509 --> 00:00:07.581
Here's why that breaks reinforcement learning.

00:00:09.933 --> 00:00:13.691
The idea: train only on samples the server recorded, and pull the

00:00:13.691 --> 00:00:16.196
learner back toward the sampler that drew them.

00:00:18.508 --> 00:00:22.040
The learner keeps training while the sampler runs weights a few steps

00:00:22.040 --> 00:00:23.512
old, with its own numerics.

00:00:23.932 --> 00:00:27.750
The server records every sequence it samples, and gives each one an id.

00:00:28.170 --> 00:00:31.383
Every scored token must point back to a recorded sequence.

00:00:31.803 --> 00:00:33.620
Then the loss updates the learner.

00:00:33.620 --> 00:00:35.438
Its main term is not published.

00:00:37.192 --> 00:00:38.315
Here is the formula.

00:00:39.192 --> 00:00:41.342
Each sampled token gets its advantage.

00:00:41.762 --> 00:00:44.213
Times the push on that token's log probability.

00:00:44.808 --> 00:00:45.747
And one coefficient.

00:00:45.747 --> 00:00:48.566
That is the only thing the losses disagree about.

00:00:50.442 --> 00:00:55.355
Importance sampling follows the ratio, and so does PPO when the advantage is negative.

00:00:55.355 --> 00:00:57.811
One token can get an enormous step.

00:00:58.231 --> 00:00:59.860
Bounded coefficients cap that step.

00:00:59.860 --> 00:01:02.466
A little bias, and the run stays alive.

00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:05.524
Here is the formula.

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:10.151
The docs publish one term: the gap between sampler and learner,

00:01:10.151 --> 00:01:11.516
on each sampled token.

00:01:11.936 --> 00:01:15.131
Scaled by a small beta, it keeps the learner near the sampler.

00:01:17.092 --> 00:01:21.773
In a published study with low precision sampling, the baselines climb, then collapse.

00:01:22.193 --> 00:01:25.714
And the mismatch between sampler and trainer grows before they fall.

00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:33.002
In my small simulation, with the sampler thirty-two steps stale, the ratio-following losses collapse.

00:01:33.002 --> 00:01:34.631
The bounded ones don't.

00:01:36.433 --> 00:01:39.391
A step that grows with the ratio is what breaks.

00:01:39.391 --> 00:01:42.348
Bound the coefficient, and watch the sampler to learner gap.

00:01:44.858 --> 00:01:47.075
Drift gives every token a ratio.

00:01:47.075 --> 00:01:48.552
Unbounded coefficients blow up.

00:01:48.552 --> 00:01:52.986
And Stable Reinforce publishes its K L term, but not its core.

00:01:53.406 --> 00:01:55.465
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:55.465 --> 00:01:56.054
I'm Cleat.

00:01:56.054 --> 00:01:56.348
Bye!
